.TH SH-GPU 1
.SH NAME
gpu \- shell script interface to gpu(2)'s backend command language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load gpu

.B ${gpu
.B available
.B }
.br
.B ${gpu
.B new
.B }
.br
.B gpu
.I id
.B device cpu|gpu
.br
.B gpu
.I id
.B precision f32|f64
.br
.B gpu
.I id
.B resident on|off
.br
.B ${gpu
.I id
.B device|precision|resident|lasterror
.B }
.br
.B gpu del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gpu
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B Backend
command language \(em which device a
.IR krylov (2)
matvec runs on, a property of the machine, independent of
.IR mesh (2)'s
geometry vocabulary and
.IR krylov (2)'s
algorithm vocabulary.
.PP
Most scripts reach
.I gpu(2)
indirectly, through
.IR fem (2)'s
own
.B backend
spec line (see
.IR sh-fem (1))
rather than building a
.B Backend
handle directly here \(em this module exists for the cases that do:
checking
.B ${gpu
.B available}
before deciding whether a script's own workload is worth the trouble,
or building a
.B Backend
to hand off explicitly.
.PP
.B ${gpu new}
allocates a handle wrapping a fresh
.IR gpu (2)
.B Backend
(default
.BR "device cpu" ),
and yields it.
.BR Device ,
.BR precision ,
and
.B resident
each call
.IR gpu (2)'s
own
.B Backend.cmd
unchanged; see
.IR gpu (2)
for what each word means.
.B ${gpu
.I id
.BR device|precision|resident }
read the handle's current settings back;
.B ${gpu
.I id
.BR lasterror }
reads the empty string unless the backend's last
.B apply
call (via
.IR fem (2)'s
own
.B solve ,
typically) had to fall back to a different device than requested.
.B ${gpu
.BR available }
is
.IR gpu (2)'s
own real availability check, not a promise \(em see
.IR gpu (2)
for what it means for this to read 0.
.B Del
frees one or more handles.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/gpu.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpu (2),
.IR fem (2),
.IR krylov (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-fem (1)
